  <!-- ==========================================
       SECTION 3 — WEEK OF PRAYER REFLECTIONS
  =========================================== -->
  <section id="reflections">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="reflections-inner">

        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-label reveal">WOP Reflections</span>
          <h2 class="reveal reveal-delay-1">Week of Prayer<br/><em>Day by Day</em></h2>
          <p class="reveal reveal-delay-2">Five days of sermons under the theme <em style="color:var(--gold);">Transformed for Eternity</em> — reflecting on Jacob, Rahab, Zacchaeus, the Samaritan woman, and Peter.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="day-cards">

          <!-- DAY 1 -->
          <div class="day-card reveal">
            <div class="day-card-header">
              <span class="day-badge">Day 1</span>
              <h4>The Blessing of Brokenness</h4>
            </div>
            <blockquote>"It is better to walk with a limp in God's will than to run freely in the wrong direction."</blockquote>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold-light);font-size:0.75rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;">Topic: Jacob's Transformation &nbsp;·&nbsp; Sermon by Bro. John Paul G. Bilinario</strong></p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
              Bro. Bilinario's sermon on Jacob was a strong warning about the dangers of relying only on ourselves. He compared Jacob's tricky nature with his later surrender to God — teaching me that while being smart might get us what we want, it often costs us our peace.
            </p>
            <p>
              The most important part for me was about how God meets us in our hardest moments. Jacob met God at the river Jabbok when he was alone and afraid. This shows that God often removes distractions so we can see who we really are. Pain and being alone are not always punishments — often, they are the only things loud enough to make us listen to God.
            </p>
            <p>
              This sermon changed how I see my struggles. Instead of seeing problems as obstacles, I now see them as chances for God to change me. Like Jacob, I often rely on my own plans instead of trusting God completely. I commit to embracing my "Jabbok moments" — those times when I feel alone and uncertain — and surrendering to God's transformation. True strength comes not from being perfect, but from depending fully on Him. I am ready to walk with a limp if it means walking with God.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- DAY 2 -->
          <div class="day-card reveal reveal-delay-1">
            <div class="day-card-header">
              <span class="day-badge">Day 2</span>
              <h4>The Scarlet Thread of Redemption</h4>
            </div>
            <blockquote>"Being saved is not about who we are or what we have done — it is about applying the blood of Jesus to our lives."</blockquote>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold-light);font-size:0.75rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;">Topic: Rahab and the Two Witnesses &nbsp;·&nbsp; Sermon by Bro. Jes Lejano</strong></p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
              Bro. Lejano's sermon connected the history of Rahab with our spiritual life today. The two spies were not just scouts — they were symbols of the Old and New Testaments. Just as Rahab had to hide these men to save her life, the Church must hide the Word of God in her heart to survive in a wicked world.
            </p>
            <p>
              The most powerful image was the Red Rope. In a city about to be destroyed, that single red rope was the only thing that saved Rahab's house — a symbol of the blood of Jesus. Rahab had a sinful past, but she was saved because she used the rope. Like her, we are living in a world that will end, but we have a way out: holding onto the Word of God and the Blood of Jesus.
            </p>
            <p>
              This sermon gave me two daily habits: <em>the Red Rope</em> — daily repentance, being cleaned each day by asking for forgiveness — and <em>the Hidden Spies</em> — daily Bible study, hiding God's Word in my heart, not just owning a Bible.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- DAY 3 -->
          <div class="day-card reveal reveal-delay-2">
            <div class="day-card-header">
              <span class="day-badge">Day 3</span>
              <h4>Faith Begins When Pride Ends</h4>
            </div>
            <blockquote>"Faith begins when pride ends. Sometimes our fear of people blocks our view of God."</blockquote>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold-light);font-size:0.75rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;">Topic: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector &nbsp;·&nbsp; Sermon by Bro. Dodon Lavisto</strong></p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
              Bro. Lavisto's sermon showed a clear path of spiritual change through five stages — from being rejected to being redeemed. The most striking point was that Zacchaeus, a rich and powerful man, climbed a tree. That picture of a grown man doing something shameful to get a better view made me ask myself: am I too worried about what others think to truly seek Jesus?
            </p>
            <p>
              What moved me most was the moment of calling. Jesus invited Himself to Zacchaeus's house <em>before</em> Zacchaeus changed his life. The world tells us to fix ourselves before we are accepted. Jesus does the opposite — He offers His presence first, and then change follows. I do not need to be perfect to come to God. I just need to open the door.
            </p>
            <p>
              Zacchaeus's story challenges my own pride. There are times I let my reputation hold me back from fully seeking Jesus. But this sermon also reminded me that true transformation must show in real actions. Like Zacchaeus, my faith must move beyond words to a changed heart that is visible to others.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- DAY 4 -->
          <div class="day-card reveal reveal-delay-3">
            <div class="day-card-header">
              <span class="day-badge">Day 4</span>
              <h4>The Well of Grace</h4>
            </div>
            <blockquote>"Jesus meets us before the change — because He causes the change."</blockquote>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold-light);font-size:0.75rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;">Topic: The Samaritan Woman &nbsp;·&nbsp; Sermon by Bro. Vilyfredo De Guzman</strong></p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
              Bro. De Guzman shared his own story of being a former gangster and drug addict, connecting it powerfully to the Samaritan woman. Jesus met the woman at noon — the hottest, most shameful hour — which showed me that He purposely goes into our places of shame to save us. He challenged my belief that I need to clean up before coming to God.
            </p>
            <p>
              The difference between <em>sin</em> and <em>thirst</em> was eye-opening. Bro. De Guzman admitted it wasn't drugs that ruined his life — it was his thirst for acceptance. Similarly, the woman's problem was her emptiness. This made me examine my own thirsts: what am I using to fill my heart? Jesus exposes these things not to condemn, but to free us — offering Living Water so we stop drinking from things that leave us empty.
            </p>
            <p>
              The most powerful image was the woman leaving her jar behind to run back to the city. That jar was her old burdens and past life. This sermon was a call to surrender. I realized I am carrying a water jar of my own plans and mistakes — and today's message invited me to leave it with Jesus.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- DAY 5 -->
          <div class="day-card reveal reveal-delay-4">
            <div class="day-card-header">
              <span class="day-badge">Day 5</span>
              <h4>The Look of Love</h4>
            </div>
            <blockquote>"When I repeat the same sins, Jesus looks at me not to condemn me — but to call me back."</blockquote>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold-light);font-size:0.75rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;">Topic: Peter — From Fear to Faithfulness &nbsp;·&nbsp; Sermon by Bro. John Ray Fabelia</strong></p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
              The final sermon spoke to me deeply because like Peter, my promises to God are often louder than my actions. Fear makes us forget our identity — Peter was brave, yet fear of a servant girl made him act as if he didn't know Jesus. I realized I also have moments where I deny my faith by following the crowd instead of doing what is right.
            </p>
            <p>
              The most moving part was the look Jesus gave Peter after the denial. I always thought it was a look of anger. But Bro. Fabelia said it was a look of love and sadness. That changes everything. And in John 21, Jesus didn't need Peter's apologies or explanations — He needed Peter's heart. Three times: "Do you love me?" Restoration is not about explaining why we failed. It is about saying we love Him.
            </p>
            <p>
              This sermon convinced me that our story does not end with failure. I want to leave this Week of Prayer with that assurance. I am <em>Transformed for Eternity</em> — not because I never fail, but because His grace always restores me. Peter went from a coward to a leader. So can I.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
