            <h3>What did you learn about yourself through your CWTS service experience?</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-a">
            <!-- REPLACE: Your answer to Q1 -->
            <p>
              I learned that I am more capable than I give myself credit for — and also more impatient than I would like to admit. The service hours forced me to slow down. When you're sweeping a long corridor at seven in the morning, there is no way to rush it. You either do the whole job or you leave it half-done. That patient consistency — seeing it through without shortcuts — was something I had to deliberately choose each time.
            </p>
            <p>
              I also learned that I deeply value purpose in my tasks. Knowing that a clean hallway means my classmates walk in a dignified space — that made the broom feel lighter. Purpose transforms labor into service.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q2 -->
        <div class="qa-item reveal reveal-delay-1">
          <div class="qa-q">
            <span class="q-mark">Q</span>
            <h3>How has CWTS changed the way you see your role in your community and future career?</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-a">
            <!-- REPLACE: Your answer to Q2 -->
            <p>
              CWTS has fundamentally reframed how I think about what it means to be an IT professional. Before, I thought my impact would come from lines of code and technical solutions — and that's still true, but it's incomplete. The most sophisticated system in the world means nothing if the person building it has no regard for the people it will serve.
            </p>
            <p>
              Sweeping floors alongside classmates taught me that community is built by people who show up. That is the role I want to carry into my career: an IT professional who sees technology as a vehicle for genuine service — not a goal in itself, but a means to improve lives, starting with the community closest to me.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Q3 -->
        <div class="qa-item reveal reveal-delay-2">
          <div class="qa-q">
            <span class="q-mark">Q</span>
            <h3>If you could speak to your future self ten years from now, what would you say about these 120 hours?</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-a">
            <!-- REPLACE: Your answer to Q3 -->
            <p>
              I would say: don't forget where you started. Before the career, before the credentials, before the projects and the systems — there were 120 hours of quiet, unglamorous, faithful work. Hours that no one applauded loudly, but that shaped you in ways you are still discovering.
            </p>
            <p>
              Remember the early mornings. Remember the weight of a full garbage bag and the satisfaction of an empty bin. Remember that service is not beneath you — it is the very foundation of everything worthwhile you will build. The ten-years-from-now version of you will have the tools. Don't let her forget the hands that learned to serve before they learned to code.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div><!-- end qa-wrapper -->

